vm-6 — Closures + uniform inter-calling (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

Make compiled closures work and make every function kind inter-callable.

WHAT
- Closure conversion: for a local LAMBDA / FLET / LABELS that captures outer lexical
  variables, the analyzer (vm-3) marks captured vars; codegen boxes them (a shared
  cell) or copies them into an UPVALUE vector.  MAKE-CLOSURE tmpl-i builds a closure
  object = { compiled-function template, captured environment (display of frames or
  upvalue vector) }.  LOAD-UPVAL / STORE-UPVAL access captured cells.  Decide box-
  per-mutated-captured-var vs a captured-frame display (display is simpler; box only
  variables that are both captured AND SETQ'd).
- Uniform APPLY: one C entry `bocl_apply(callable, args, nargs, results)` that
  dispatches on the object kind:
    BUILT-IN-FUNCTION -> kernel_apply_built_in
    compiled function/closure -> bocl_vm_run
    tree-walked FUNCTION/CLOSURE -> kernel_apply_function/closure (evale body)
  so bytecode can call built-ins and not-yet-compiled tree-walked functions and vice
  versa, with correct &optional/&rest/&key arg processing (share the existing
  parameter-binding for the tree-walked/kernel sides; the VM binds required args
  fast and falls back to the general binder for &optional/&key).
- This gives INCREMENTAL migration: uncompiled code keeps working; only compiled
  functions run in the VM.

ACCEPTANCE: adder-returning closures, mutable captured counters, mutually-recursive
LABELS, a compiled fn calling a tree-walked fn and a built-in and vice versa;
&optional/&rest/&key on a compiled fn; parity with the tree-walker.

DEPENDS ON: vm-4 (and vm-5 for LABELS control).
